# Slide 3 - Specifications Driven Development (3 min)

One of the biggest lessons for me was how the engineering effort shifted.

Traditionally, we spend most of our time writing code.

With AI, I found the work shifting toward something closer to:

40% context setting,
20% generation and iteration,
and 40% review and verification.

Another pattern I noticed was what I call the "ask it again" loop.

When requirements or specifications are unclear, we tend to keep asking AI the same question in different ways, generating more responses, consuming more tokens, and spending more time.

In many cases, the real issue is not the model. The issue is that the specification is not clear enough.

That's why shifting towrads specifications-driven development helped!

"What I noticed was that better specifications consistently produced better outcomes. The more clearly I defined the problem up front, the fewer iterations I needed from AI."

And even when AI helps generate the solution, making it production ready still requires the same engineering principles we have always relied on.


We have prod readiness specs defined well for resiliency, rollback plans, observability, security, and operational controls.


The biggest takeaway here was that AI can help us move much faster, but human review, testing, and engineering judgment still matter just as much.
